  q(LTI,'A tourniquet is in place and bleeding has stopped. Should it be loosened to check the wound?','No, leave it tight',['Yes, every 5 minutes','Yes, every 10 minutes','Yes, to check the pulse'],'A','Only trained medical staff should remove a tourniquet, since loosening it can restart bleeding.',FAST),
  q(LTI,'What part of a tourniquet is twisted to tighten it?','Windlass',['Buckle','Velcro tail','Clip'],'B','Twisting the rod tightens the band until bleeding stops, then the rod is locked in place.',FAST),
  q(LTI,'An unresponsive victim is breathing and has no suspected spinal injury. How should they be placed?','In the recovery position',['Flat on the back with legs up','Sitting up against a wall','Face down with arms out'],'C','Lying on the side lets fluids drain from the mouth and keeps the airway open.',CERT),
  q(LTI,'After direct pressure, which step helps maintain pressure on a bleeding arm wound?','Apply a pressure bandage',['Remove the dressing','Pour water on it','Raise the feet only'],'D','A pressure bandage frees the rescuer’s hands to treat other victims.',CERT),
  q(LTI,'Which sign suggests life-threatening bleeding?','Blood pooling or soaking clothes',['A small scrape that stops','Mild bruising on the upper arm','A dry scab on the knee'],'A','Spurting blood, pooling on the ground or blood-soaked clothing all need immediate action.',FAST),
  q(LTI,'Why must a tourniquet be applied directly to the skin, not over loose objects in pockets?','Objects can stop it from tightening',['It looks more professional','It is easier to read the time','It keeps the clothing clean'],'B','Bulky items under the band leave gaps, so the artery may not be fully compressed.',FAST),
  q(MED,'Where should CERT members place the bodies of victims who have died?','A temporary morgue area',['The medical treatment area','The command post','The supply area'],'C','The morgue should be kept separate and secure, and bodies treated with dignity.',CERT),
  q(MED,'When water and sewer systems fail, what should a CERT do for sanitation?','Set up latrines away from water',['Use the nearest stream','Bury waste beside the kitchen','Ignore it for a week'],'D','Latrines should be away from food and water areas to prevent the spread of disease.',CERT),
  q(MED,'How should a nosebleed be treated?','Lean forward and pinch the nose',['Tilt the head far back','Lie flat on the back','Blow the nose hard to clear it'],'A','Leaning forward keeps blood from running down the throat and causing vomiting.',CERT),
  q(MED,'A victim has dry chemical on the skin. What should be done first?','Brush off, then flush with water',['Rub it in with a towel','Cover it with butter or oil','Wrap it tightly with a bandage'],'B','Brushing first keeps water from activating more of the dry chemical on the skin.',CERT),
  q(MED,'A victim has an object stuck in one eye. What should the rescuer do?','Stabilize it and cover both eyes',['Pull the object out at once','Rinse it out with soda','Cover only the injured eye'],'C','Covering both eyes reduces movement, since the eyes move together.',CERT),
  q(MED,'A victim has a mild sprained ankle. What does the RICE method stand for?','Rest, ice, compression, elevation',['Run, ice, cool, exercise','Rest, inspect, cut and elevate it','Rub, ice, carry, exercise'],'D','RICE reduces pain and swelling; ice should be wrapped, not placed on bare skin.',CERT),
  q(MED,'How should CERT members splint a suspected dislocation?','In the position it was found',['Pull it back into place','Straighten it fully first','Leave it unsupported'],'A','Trying to reset a joint in the field can damage nearby nerves and blood vessels.',CERT),
  q(MED,'A victim is sweating heavily, pale and dizzy on a hot day but alert. What is likely?','Heat exhaustion',['Heat stroke','Exertional frostbite','Hypothermia'],'B','Move them to a cooler place, loosen clothing and give small sips of water.',CERT),
  q(MED,'How should frostbitten fingers be rewarmed?','In warm, not hot, water',['By rubbing with snow','Near an open flame','With a hair dryer on high'],'C','Rubbing damages frozen tissue, and numb skin can easily burn from direct heat.',CERT),
  q(MED,'Why should CERT members keep records on each victim in the treatment area?','To track care and pass on information',['To bill the victim’s family later','To post updates on social media','To rank the rescuers by speed'],'D','Documentation helps professional responders continue care and find missing people.',CERT),
  q(PSY,'After a disaster, a young child starts bedwetting and thumb sucking again. What is this?','Regression',['Denial','Rebellion','Recovery'],'A','Regression is a common stress reaction, and routines and comfort help children recover.',CERT),
  q(PSY,'Which is a healthy way for CERT members to reduce their own stress during a response?','Eat, drink water and take breaks',['Skip meals to work faster','Avoid talking to teammates','Work double shifts with no rest'],'B','Rescuers who rest, rotate and stay fed make fewer mistakes and recover better.',CERT),
  q(FIRE,'Which class of fire involves energized electrical equipment?','Class C',['Class A','Class B','Class K'],'C','Never use water on an electrical fire, because it can carry the current to the rescuer.',CERT),
  q(FIRE,'Which type of extinguisher can be used on Class A, B and C fires?','Dry chemical ABC',['Pressurized water','Wet chemical','Class D powder'],'D','Multipurpose ABC extinguishers are common in homes and offices for that reason.',CERT),
  q(FIRE,'What three elements make up the fire triangle?','Heat, fuel and oxygen',['Smoke, ash and flame','Wood, paper and gas','Wind, rain and heat'],'A','Removing any one side of the triangle, such as smothering the oxygen, puts the fire out.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'A building has superficial damage, such as broken windows and small cracks. What may CERT do?','Search it with normal caution',['Never enter at all','Enter only by helicopter','Mark it for demolition'],'B','Light damage is generally safe for a CERT search, but hazards should still be watched for.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'A building has moderate damage. What may CERT members do?','Quick in-and-out rescues only',['Full, slow searches of each room','Lift heavy structural beams','Live inside until help comes'],'C','Moderate damage calls for great caution and a minimum time inside the structure.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'Where are trapped victims most likely to survive inside a collapsed building?','In void spaces',['Under heavy beams','In open hallways','On the roof'],'D','Voids form near sturdy furniture and walls, giving people room to breathe.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'A wall falls with one end on the floor and the other still resting on a wall. What void is this?','Lean-to void',['Pancake void','V-shaped void','A-frame void'],'A','Victims may be sheltered in the triangular space under the leaning section.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'Which one-person method moves an unresponsive victim using a sheet under them?','Blanket drag',['Chair carry','Fireman’s carry','Two-person lift'],'B','Pulling along the body’s long axis helps protect the spine during the move.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'In the CERT search marking, what goes in the bottom quadrant?','Number of victims found',['CERT team identification','Time in and time out','Hazards found inside'],'C','Victims are recorded as live or dead, and where they were moved, if they were.',CERT),
  q(SAR,'Two rescuers lock hands to form a seat for a conscious victim who can hold on. What carry is this?','Two-handed seat carry',['One-person arm carry','Fireman’s shoulder carry','Blanket drag method'],'B','The victim puts their arms around the rescuers’ shoulders, so the method works only for alert victims.',CERT),
  q(TER,'What does the acronym CBRNE stand for?','Chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear, explosive',['Cyber, bomb, riot, nuclear and emergency events','Crime, bio, rescue, natural, event','Chemical, burn, radio, noise, electric'],'D','These are the main types of weapons that could be used in a terrorist attack.',CERT),
  q(TER,'A CERT member sees an unattended, suspicious package. What should they do?','Leave it and report it',['Open it to look inside','Move it to a quiet area','Shake it to listen'],'A','Keep people away, avoid using radios nearby and let trained responders handle it.',CERT)
